From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 04 Sep 2026 15:58:51 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] xen/mapcache: keep foreign bucket heads mapped when unlocked

Since the grant-mapping series (9.1, "xen: mapcache: Unmap first entries in
buckets") xen_invalidate_map_cache_entry_unlocked() munmaps the head entry
of a bucket as soon as its lock count reaches zero. Before that, the head
entry stayed mapped (the "|| pentry == NULL" early return) and only chained
entries were torn down, which is what made the mapcache a cache for the
common case of one entry per bucket.

The consequence for a device that maps guest pages one 4 KiB entry at a
time is that every dma_memory_map()/dma_memory_unmap() pair is now a
privcmd foreign-map hypercall plus a munmap. Measured on RPi4 DomD with
qemu 10.0.2 while virtio-media copied a 942 KB decoded frame into guest
USERPTR pages: about 6 ms per frame in the map/unmap path for 230 entries,
against 0.6 ms for the memcpy itself. Every DMA-capable virtio device in
the same process (blk, net, gpu) pays the same per-page cost.

The xen-troops fork carried the pre-9.1 behaviour back as a workaround
(8e6a6725, "xen: mapcache: leave first in buckets unmapped") and reverted
it in the commit this recipe pins (f460a035), for two stated reasons: with
the fork's 64 KiB buckets (0bafd789, "xen-mapcache: adjust bucket size")
the workaround is not needed, and keeping heads mapped leaves a lot of
memory to unmap when a domain is destroyed, so the domain takes long to
reboot. The first reason does not hold for the per-page mapping above: the
6 ms per frame was measured on the pinned source, 64 KiB buckets included.
The second one applies to this patch too, for DomA, whose virtio devices
all use foreign mappings: how much longer DomA's destroy and reboot take
with it has not been measured.

Restore the pre-9.1 behaviour for foreign mappings only. Grant mappings
keep being unmapped at lock zero, because the guest must be able to revoke
a grant, which is the reason upstream changed this in the first place.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
diff --git a/hw/xen/xen-mapcache.c b/hw/xen/xen-mapcache.c
index c5616ca54d1..dccf33045f8 100644
--- a/hw/xen/xen-mapcache.c
+++ b/hw/xen/xen-mapcache.c
@@ -585,6 +585,26 @@ static void xen_invalidate_map_cache_entry_unlocked(MapCache *mc,
     if (entry->lock > 0) {
         return;
     }
+    /*
+     * Keep the head entry of a bucket mapped once its lock drops to zero,
+     * unless it is a grant. This is what every release up to 9.0 did
+     * (upstream's "|| pentry == NULL" early return, removed in 9.1), and it
+     * is what makes this a cache: a foreign mapping that is torn down on
+     * every unlock is re-created by a privcmd hypercall on the next
+     * address_space_map() of the same page.
+     * Upstream started unmapping head entries so that transient grant
+     * mappings can be released (the guest has to be able to revoke them);
+     * a foreign mapping of guest RAM has no such requirement.
+     *
+     * Measured on RPi4 DomD, qemu 10.0.2, virtio-media copying a 942 KB
+     * decoded frame into guest USERPTR pages one 4 KiB entry at a time:
+     * about 6 ms per frame in dma_memory_map()/dma_memory_unmap(), i.e.
+     * 230 map+unmap hypercall pairs, where the memcpy itself is 0.6 ms.
+     * Every DMA-capable device (virtio-blk, -net, -gpu) pays the same tax.
+     */
+    if (pentry == NULL && !(entry->flags & XEN_MAPCACHE_ENTRY_GRANT)) {
+        return;
+    }
 
     ram_block_notify_remove(entry->vaddr_base, entry->size, entry->size);
     if (entry->flags & XEN_MAPCACHE_ENTRY_GRANT) {
